1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,379
This is ECE 358, video 24, the last video: switches, datacenter networks, and a day in the life of a web request. Chapter 6, slides 65 to 100.

2
00:00:14,829 --> 00:00:28,589
You will fill a switch table frame by frame, trace frames through several switches, compare switches and routers, describe datacenter networks, and list every protocol in one web request, in order.

3
00:00:29,039 --> 00:00:35,225
A layer 2 switch is a link-layer device. It stores the Ethernet frames that arrive.

4
00:00:35,675 --> 00:00:50,694
It examines the destination MAC address of each frame. Then it forwards the frame selectively, on one or more outgoing links. On a half-duplex port, it uses CSMA/CD to access the link.

5
00:00:51,144 --> 00:01:00,317
A switch is transparent. The hosts do not know that the switch is there. A host addresses frames to the other host, not to the switch.

6
00:01:00,767 --> 00:01:06,207
A switch is plug-and-play and self-learning. Switches do not need to be configured.

7
00:01:06,657 --> 00:01:20,353
Here is a switch with six interfaces, numbered 1 to 6. Each host has a dedicated, direct connection to the switch. Ethernet runs on each link in full duplex, so there are no collisions.

8
00:01:20,803 --> 00:01:27,267
So A can send to A prime, and B can send to B prime, at the same time. There is no collision.

9
00:01:27,717 --> 00:01:30,789
Each switch port is its own collision domain.

10
00:01:31,239 --> 00:01:42,503
But A to A prime and C to A prime cannot happen at the same time. Both frames must leave on port 4. So the switch buffers one frame until port 4 is free.

11
00:01:42,953 --> 00:01:49,780
How does the switch know that A prime is reachable through interface 4, and B prime through interface 5?

12
00:01:50,230 --> 00:02:02,177
Each switch has a switch table. Each entry has three parts: the MAC address of a host, the interface that reaches the host, and a TTL. It looks like a routing table.

13
00:02:02,627 --> 00:02:09,005
How does the switch create these entries? It needs no routing protocol. The switch learns them.

14
00:02:09,455 --> 00:02:15,045
The switch table is empty. A frame from A to A prime arrives on interface 1.

15
00:02:15,495 --> 00:02:22,791
When a frame arrives, the switch learns the location of the sender. A is on the incoming link, interface 1.

16
00:02:23,241 --> 00:02:34,611
The switch records this sender and location pair in its table: A, interface 1, with a TTL of 60. When the TTL ends, the entry is removed.

17
00:02:35,061 --> 00:02:43,787
Here is the full algorithm. When a frame arrives: step 1, record the incoming port and the MAC address of the sending host.

18
00:02:44,237 --> 00:02:48,909
Step 2. Index the switch table with the destination MAC address.

19
00:02:49,359 --> 00:02:53,113
Step 3. Check if there is an entry for the destination.

20
00:02:53,563 --> 00:03:05,915
If there is an entry, check if the destination is on the same port that the frame came from. If yes, drop the frame. If no, forward the frame on the interface in the entry.

21
00:03:06,365 --> 00:03:13,832
If there is no entry, flood. That is, forward the frame on all interfaces except the arriving interface.

22
00:03:14,282 --> 00:03:27,338
Slide 72. The table is empty. Frame 1 goes from A to A prime. The location of A prime is unknown, so the switch floods the frame on ports 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6.

23
00:03:27,788 --> 00:03:32,695
The switch learns that A is on interface 1, and adds A, 1, 60.

24
00:03:33,145 --> 00:03:43,982
Frame 2 is the reply, from A prime to A. The location of A is known: interface 1. So the switch sends the frame on just one link, interface 1.

25
00:03:44,432 --> 00:03:50,811
The switch also learns that A prime is on interface 4. Other hosts did not see frame 2.

26
00:03:51,261 --> 00:04:05,810
A layer 2 switch has a finite number of ports. Common sizes are 8, 16, 24, and 48 ports. For hundreds of hosts, you connect switches together. Here four switches connect nine hosts.

27
00:04:06,260 --> 00:04:16,735
A sends to G. How does S1 know that it must forward the frame to S4, and S4 to S3? Self-learning, exactly as with one switch.

28
00:04:17,185 --> 00:04:30,070
Note the many-to-1 mapping. In S1, the hosts D, E, F, G, H, and I are all reached through the same port, the link to S4. So many MAC addresses map to the same port.

29
00:04:30,520 --> 00:04:37,987
Slide 74. C sends a frame to I, and then I responds to C. All four tables start empty.

30
00:04:38,437 --> 00:04:47,247
Frame 1, C to I. S1 learns C on C's link. I is unknown, so S1 floods: to A, to B, and to S4.

31
00:04:47,697 --> 00:04:52,305
S4 learns C through S1, and floods to S2 and S3.

32
00:04:52,755 --> 00:05:03,443
S2 and S3 learn C through S4, and both flood. I receives the frame. D, E, F, G, and H also receive it and discard it.

33
00:05:03,893 --> 00:05:13,728
Frame 2, I to C. S3 learns I on I's link. C is in S3's table, through S4, so S3 forwards only to S4.

34
00:05:14,178 --> 00:05:27,490
S4 learns I through S3. It forwards only to S1. S1 learns I through S4, and forwards only to C. S2 does not see frame 2, so S2 does not learn I.

35
00:05:27,940 --> 00:05:42,831
Slide 75 shows a small institutional network: a switch hierarchy inside one IP subnet. The mail server and the web server connect to the top switch. The hosts connect to the lower switches.

36
00:05:43,281 --> 00:05:53,926
A router connects the subnet to the external network. Inside the subnet, switches forward frames. To leave the subnet, a datagram goes to the router.

37
00:05:54,376 --> 00:06:02,035
Switches and routers are both store-and-forward devices. Here a datagram goes through a switch, then a router.

38
00:06:02,485 --> 00:06:19,850
A router is a network-layer device. It examines network-layer headers. A switch is a link-layer device. It examines only link-layer headers. So the datagram goes up to the network layer in the router, but only to the link layer in the switch.

39
00:06:20,300 --> 00:06:32,887
Both have forwarding tables. A router computes its routing table with routing algorithms and IP addresses. A switch learns its table with flooding and learning, and MAC addresses.

40
00:06:33,337 --> 00:06:44,451
Datacenter networks connect tens to hundreds of thousands of hosts. The hosts are closely coupled and close together. Examples are Amazon, YouTube, and Google.

41
00:06:44,901 --> 00:06:58,768
There are three challenges. Many applications each serve massive numbers of clients. Communication must be reliable. And the load must be balanced, to avoid processing, networking, and data bottlenecks.

42
00:06:59,218 --> 00:07:05,554
From the bottom up: server racks. Each rack holds 20 to 40 server blades, the hosts.

43
00:07:06,004 --> 00:07:15,284
Each rack has one top of rack switch, or TOR switch. It connects to the blades at 40 to 100 gigabits per second.

44
00:07:15,734 --> 00:07:20,555
Tier-2 switches each connect to about 16 TOR switches below.

45
00:07:21,005 --> 00:07:25,592
Tier-1 switches each connect to about 16 tier-2 switches below.

46
00:07:26,042 --> 00:07:34,661
At the top, border routers connect the datacenter to the outside. Slide 80 shows the Facebook F16 topology.

47
00:07:35,111 --> 00:07:45,095
This is a smaller version of slide 81. Each tier-2 switch connects to every tier-1 switch, and each rack connects to two tier-2 switches.

48
00:07:45,545 --> 00:07:52,947
So there are multiple routing paths between two racks. Here is one path from rack 1 to rack 6, in red.

49
00:07:53,397 --> 00:08:03,232
Here is a second path, in blue. It uses different switches, so the two paths are disjoint. Multiple paths increase throughput and reliability.

50
00:08:03,682 --> 00:08:09,997
A load balancer does application-layer routing. It receives the external client requests.

51
00:08:10,447 --> 00:08:14,265
It directs the workload to servers inside the datacenter.

52
00:08:14,715 --> 00:08:21,691
It returns the results to the external client. So the client does not see the inside of the datacenter.

53
00:08:22,141 --> 00:08:32,445
At the link layer: RoCE, remote DMA over converged Ethernet. Data moves between the memories of two servers, without their CPUs.

54
00:08:32,895 --> 00:08:49,983
At the transport layer: explicit congestion notification, ECN, is used in congestion control, for example DCTCP and DCQCN. There are also tests with hop-by-hop, backpressure, congestion control.

55
00:08:50,433 --> 00:08:56,321
For routing and management, SDN is widely used inside and among datacenters.

56
00:08:56,771 --> 00:09:09,635
Related services and data are placed as close as possible, for example in the same rack or a nearby rack. This minimizes the traffic through the tier-2 and tier-1 switches.

57
00:09:10,085 --> 00:09:25,083
Now we put it all together: every protocol in one simple scenario, on the network of slide 93. There is a school network, the Comcast network with a DNS server, and Google's network with the web server.

58
00:09:25,533 --> 00:09:29,117
A student attaches a laptop to the campus network.

59
00:09:29,567 --> 00:09:34,601
Then the student requests the web page www.google.com.

60
00:09:35,051 --> 00:09:49,729
Slide 86 shows everything in one picture. On the laptop, the DHCP client runs on UDP port 68. The web browser runs HTTP on TCP, from a free port.

61
00:09:50,179 --> 00:10:09,955
On the servers: the DHCP server runs on UDP port 67. The DNS server runs on UDP port 53. The web server runs HTTP on TCP port 80. Under each, there are IP, the link layer, and the physical layer.

62
00:10:10,405 --> 00:10:23,738
Between them are switches that learn, the first-hop router, and ARP. Routing works inside each autonomous system with OSPF or RIP, and between autonomous systems with BGP.

63
00:10:24,188 --> 00:10:39,484
Step 1. The laptop needs its own IP address. It also needs the IP address of the first-hop router, the address of the DNS server, and the network mask. It gets all of them with DHCP.

64
00:10:39,934 --> 00:10:58,174
The DHCP request is encapsulated in UDP, then in IP, then in an Ethernet frame. The DHCP client uses UDP port 68 and an IP broadcast. The frame's destination MAC is the broadcast address.

65
00:10:58,624 --> 00:11:17,013
The switch floods the broadcast frame, and it learns where the laptop is. The router runs the DHCP server and receives the frame. It demultiplexes: Ethernet to IP, IP to UDP, UDP to DHCP, on port 67.

66
00:11:17,463 --> 00:11:31,543
The DHCP ACK holds the client's IP address, the first-hop router's IP address, and the DNS server's name and IP address. The switch forwards it to the laptop, as it learned.

67
00:11:31,993 --> 00:11:38,159
Now the client has an IP address, and knows the DNS server and its first-hop router.

68
00:11:38,609 --> 00:11:54,246
Step 2. The laptop needs the IP address of www.google.com, so it uses DNS. To send the DNS frame to the router, the laptop needs the MAC address of the router interface.

69
00:11:54,696 --> 00:12:01,096
So the laptop runs ARP. The ARP query is broadcast on the LAN. The router receives it.

70
00:12:01,546 --> 00:12:11,957
The router replies with an ARP reply that gives the MAC address of its interface. Now the laptop can send the frame that holds the DNS query.

71
00:12:12,407 --> 00:12:31,415
Step 3. The laptop sends the DNS query to the DNS application at UDP port 53 on the DNS server. It knows the server's IP address from DHCP. The LAN switch forwards the frame from the laptop to the first-hop router.

72
00:12:31,865 --> 00:12:43,683
The datagram goes through the Comcast network to the DNS server. The routers use tables that RIP, OSPF, IS-IS, and BGP created.

73
00:12:44,133 --> 00:12:57,083
At the DNS server, the datagram is demultiplexed to DNS. The DNS server replies to the client with the IP address of www.google.com.

74
00:12:57,533 --> 00:13:10,589
Step 4. To send the HTTP request, the browser first asks TCP to open a connection, from its IP address and port to the web server's IP address, port 80.

75
00:13:11,039 --> 00:13:18,740
TCP sends a SYN segment, step 1 of the three-way handshake, routed between domains to the web server.

76
00:13:19,190 --> 00:13:29,089
The web server responds with a SYN plus ACK segment, step 2. The laptop answers with an ACK. The TCP connection is now established.

77
00:13:29,539 --> 00:13:46,371
Step 5. The browser sends the HTTP request into the TCP socket. It asks for index.htm. The IP datagram with the HTTP request is routed to www.google.com.

78
00:13:46,821 --> 00:14:02,799
The web server puts the file in an HTTP reply, and asks TCP to send it. TCP puts it in a segment, and the network layer sends an IP packet to the laptop, routed back through the autonomous systems.

79
00:14:03,249 --> 00:14:07,345
The browser receives the response and displays the web page.

80
00:14:07,795 --> 00:14:17,609
Question bank. Question 56: what is the purpose of a switch table? Question 57: what is its structure? Pause the video and try it.

81
00:14:18,059 --> 00:14:28,555
Answer to 56. The switch table tells the switch on which interface to forward each frame. So the switch forwards selectively, instead of everywhere.

82
00:14:29,005 --> 00:14:40,951
Answer to 57. Each entry has the MAC address of a host, the interface that reaches the host, and a TTL. When the TTL ends, the entry is removed.

83
00:14:41,401 --> 00:14:51,961
Question 58: how does a switch construct its table? Question 59: which mapping best describes a switch table? Pause the video and try it.

84
00:14:52,411 --> 00:15:02,737
Answer to 58. By self-learning. It records the sender's MAC address and the incoming interface of each frame. It needs no configuration.

85
00:15:03,187 --> 00:15:18,568
Answer to 59. Many-to-1. Each MAC address maps to one interface, but many MAC addresses can map to the same interface. In S1, the six hosts behind S4 all map to the port toward S4.

86
00:15:19,018 --> 00:15:26,890
Question 60 gives the algorithm of slide 71 with gaps. Fill in the gaps. Pause the video and try it.

87
00:15:27,340 --> 00:15:44,065
When a frame is received at the switch. Step 1: record the incoming link and the MAC address of the sending host. Step 2: index the switch table with the destination MAC address. Step 3: if an entry is found for the destination.

88
00:15:44,515 --> 00:15:55,630
Then, if the destination is on the port the frame came from, drop the frame. Else, forward the frame on the interface in the entry. If no entry is found, flood.

89
00:15:56,080 --> 00:16:06,469
Question 61: what is flooding? Question 62: an L2 switch floods in two situations. What are they? Pause the video and try it.

90
00:16:06,919 --> 00:16:15,410
Answer to 61. Flooding means the switch forwards the frame on all interfaces except the interface where it arrived.

91
00:16:15,860 --> 00:16:27,060
Answer to 62. Situation 1: the destination MAC address is not in the switch table. This is the first frame to A prime on slide 72.

92
00:16:27,510 --> 00:16:43,659
Situation 2: the destination is the broadcast address, for example an ARP query or a DHCP request. No frame has the broadcast address as its source, so step 3 never finds an entry, and the switch floods.

93
00:16:44,109 --> 00:17:03,352
Question 63. You must connect 1000 hosts. Each L2 switch has 48 ports. How many switches do you need? Show a layout that minimizes the delay to an external server. There is no published answer, so we use the slides. Pause the video and try it.

94
00:17:03,802 --> 00:17:30,831
Count ports. n switches have 48 n ports, for 1000 hosts and 1 router port. Each link between two switches uses 2 ports, and a tree of n switches has n minus 1 such links. So 48 n must be at least 1001 plus 2 times n minus 1. That gives 46 n at least 999, so n is at least 21.7. So you need at least 22 switches.

95
00:17:31,281 --> 00:17:43,676
Layout: one root switch connects to the router, and 21 access switches connect to the root. Each access switch uses 1 port for the uplink and 47 ports for hosts.

96
00:17:44,126 --> 00:18:00,873
21 times 47 is 987 hosts. The root uses 21 ports for the access switches and 1 for the router. 26 ports are free, and 13 more hosts go on the root. So all 1000 hosts fit.

97
00:18:01,323 --> 00:18:10,133
For delay: every frame reaches the router through at most 2 store-and-forward switches. A chain of switches would add many hops.

98
00:18:10,583 --> 00:18:27,501
Question 64 uses the network of slide 73. All four switch tables start empty. C sends a frame to I, then F sends a frame to C. Part a: show the four switch tables at the end. Pause the video and try it.

99
00:18:27,951 --> 00:18:43,183
Frame 1, C to I, is as on slide 74. S1 learns C on C's link. S4 learns C through S1. S2 and S3 learn C through S4. Nobody knows I, so every switch floods.

100
00:18:43,633 --> 00:19:01,851
Frame 2, F to C. S2 learns F on F's link. S2 knows C, through S4, so it forwards only to S4. S4 learns F through S2 and forwards only to S1. S1 learns F through S4 and forwards only to C.

101
00:19:02,301 --> 00:19:15,677
So S1, S2, and S4 have two entries each: C and F. S3 has only C, because frame 2 never reaches S3. Note that no switch learns I. I never sends a frame.

102
00:19:16,127 --> 00:19:24,725
Part b: how many frames actually reach the hardware interfaces of hosts A, D, and G? Pause the video and try it.

103
00:19:25,175 --> 00:19:33,495
Frame 1, C to I, is flooded by every switch. So it reaches A through S1, D through S2, and G through S3.

104
00:19:33,945 --> 00:19:42,691
Frame 2, F to C, is forwarded only on the path S2, S4, S1, to C. It does not reach A, D, or G.

105
00:19:43,141 --> 00:19:49,392
So exactly one frame reaches each of A, D, and G, and their adapters discard it.

106
00:19:49,842 --> 00:19:54,855
Three synthesis questions that use this video. Pause the video and try them.

107
00:19:55,305 --> 00:20:08,255
Question 1. A network-layer protocol is present on hosts and routers, not on links or L2 switches. Slide 76 shows that a switch goes up only to the link layer.

108
00:20:08,705 --> 00:20:18,113
Question 2. DHCP uses UDP. The client uses port 68 and the server uses port 67.

109
00:20:18,563 --> 00:20:38,531
Question 3. The order is: DHCP, for its own IP address, the router, and the DNS server. Then ARP, for the router's MAC address. Then DNS, for the IP address of www.google.com. Then the TCP handshake, then HTTP.

110
00:20:38,981 --> 00:20:54,917
To recap. A switch is a transparent, plug-and-play, store-and-forward link-layer device. It learns from the sender of each frame. It forwards to a known destination, and floods for an unknown or broadcast destination.

111
00:20:55,367 --> 00:21:03,047
A router uses IP addresses and routing algorithms. A switch uses MAC addresses and self-learning.

112
00:21:03,497 --> 00:21:11,539
A datacenter connects racks through top of rack, tier-2, and tier-1 switches, with many paths and a load balancer.

113
00:21:11,989 --> 00:21:25,451
And one web request uses DHCP, ARP, DNS, TCP, and HTTP, in that order. This completes ECE 358. Good luck on the exam.
